 ### Box Integration for Parallel/Opposite Vectors
 Handles special cases where incident ($\tilde{\omega}_i$) and outgoing ($\tilde{\omega}_o$) light vectors are parallel or opposite, requiring separate solutions without traversing the box outline [S1].
-*   **Opposite Vectors:** If $\tilde{\omega}_i \cdot \tilde{\omega}_o < 0$, the end distance is set to zero. Box transmittance is computed using trapezoid integration with parameters $(0, t_C, d_L, 0)$ [S1].
-*   **Same Direction:** If vectors face the same direction, the end distance is the sum of distances to both exit points along the incident ($d_L$) and outgoing ($t_C$) vectors. Box transmittance is computed with parameters $(0, t_C, d_L, d_L + t_C)$ [S1].
